  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "The displacement $s$ (in meters) relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)={e}^{{t}^{2}-4t+6}$, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time measured in seconds. Find the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$",
  "worked_solution": "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative.\n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{{t}^{2}-4t+6}) \\\\  & =(2t-4){e}^{{t}^{2}-4t+6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we must solve $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ (2t-4){e}^{{t}^{2}-4t+6} & <0 \\\\ 2t-4 & <0 \\\\ 2t & <4 \\\\ t & <2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $[0,2).$",
